GAL16V8
6502 ICV

PHI2 RWB  CE    V1   V2   S0   S1   S2   A3   GND
VPB  A4   RENB  WCK  VIRQ A4   A3   A2   A1   VCC

A1.T = VIRQ*S0 + /VIRQ*V1
A1.E = VPB
A2.T = VIRQ*S1 + /VIRQ*V2
A2.E = VPB
A3.T = VIRQ*S2 + /VIRQ
A3.E = VPB
A4.T = /VIRQ
A4.E = VPB

WCK = A3*A4*/CE*/RWB*/PHI2
RENB = A3*A4*/CE*RWB*PHI2

DESCRIPTION

This is the vector address selection logic. On the main board, the 6502's
A4..1 address lines are connected to tristate buffers whose outputs are 
connected are used as A4..1 on the system bus. These buffers are in the high 
impedance state whenever VPB is low. This is designed to allow a separate
module to provide vectored interrupt support.

The V1 and V2 inputs to this logic are the 6502's (unbuffered) A1 and A2 
address lines. The S2..0 inputs represent the decoded address output of the 
priority decoder, which specifies requested IRQ vector.

THe A4..1 outputs of this logic have tristate outputs that are controlled
by VPB. The outputs are driven only when VPB is low. The logic is designed
to output the appropriate vector address based on the state of V1 and V2.
Bits A15..5 and A0 in the vector address driven directly by the 6502. Bits 
A4..1 are driven by this logic based on V1 and V2 as follows.

 V2 V1 S2 S1 S0  Vector  Description
  0  1  X  X  X  FFFA	 Non-maskable interrupt (NMI)
  1  0  X  X  X	 FFFC	 System reset (RESET)
  1  1  0  0  0  FFE0    Interrupt request 0 (IRQ0)	
  1  1  0  0  1  FFE2    Interrupt request 1 (IRQ1)	
  1  1  0  1  0  FFE4    Interrupt request 2 (IRQ2)	
  1  1  0  1  1  FFE6    Interrupt request 3 (IRQ3)	
  1  1  1  0  0  FFE8    Interrupt request 4 (IRQ4)	
  1  1  1  0  1  FFEA    Interrupt request 5 (IRQ5)	
  1  1  1  0  1  FFEC    Interrupt request 6 (IRQ6)	
  1  1  1  1  1  FFEE    Interrupt request 7 (IRQ7)	

The WCK and RENB provide a write clock signal and a read enable signal for
the mask register. Using the product of IOEN2, A3, and A4 in the derivation 
of these signals, the register is addressed as an I/O device at any address
in the range $FFD8..FFDF.
